---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import ExternalLink from "../components/ExternalLink.astro";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title="Timeline" description="Technical life of deanqx" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <Header />
    <main>
      <h1>Timeline</h1>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2025-07-26_portrait.webp"
        alt="Portrait of me"
        width={300}
      />
      <p>
        Hello my name is Dean. I'm currently studying computer science in west
        Germany.
      </p>
      <h2>September 2026</h2>
      Beginning of my computer science studies at university.
      <h2>June 2026</h2>
      <p>
        Server hosting, cluster management with Kubernetes, backups, security,
        continuous integration and this Website.
      </p>
      <div style="position: relative;">
        <h2>February 2026</h2>
        <div class="circuit-background"></div>
        <div class="image-grid">
          <img
            style="grid-row: 1;"
            src="/timeline/2026-03-05_go_kart_cockpit.webp"
            alt="Go-Kart cockpit hardware"
            width={400}
          />
          <img
            style="grid-row: 2;"
            src="/timeline/2026-02-17_pcb.png"
            alt="3D view of a Go-Kart Circuit I designed and built."
            width={400}
          />
          <img
            style="grid-row: span 2;"
            src="/timeline/2026-02-19_go_kart.webp"
            alt="Go-Kart"
            width={400}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The <ExternalLink href="https://github.com/deanqx/go-kart">
          go-kart
        </ExternalLink> project really got my love and my nerves. It was the final
        project at that school and spanned over one year. In a team of four we upgraded
        a Kettcar with typical car features using AVR microcontrollers and self-designed
        PCBs. This project had been worked on over many years and had turned into
        an undocumented mess. My role covered the cockpit, lights, and structuring
        the CAN-Bus communication. So this picked up on what I like: microcontrollers
        and a lot of C programming.
      </p>
      <h2>March 2025</h2>
      <video
        src="/timeline/2025-03-07_first_pcb.webm"
        type="video/mp4"
        loading="lazy"
        muted
        autoplay
        loop
        playinline
        width="700"></video>
      <p>
        My first self-designed PCB in school. In the previous year we learned
        the basics of electronics: voltage, current, circuits, resistance,
        capacity, inductivity and more. The PCB project extended the list with
        transistors, thyristors, diodes, and reading datasheets.
      </p>
      <h2>October 2024</h2>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2024-10-03_parade_float.webp"
        alt="Parade Float"
        width={700}
      />
      My classmates and I built a float for the Oktoberfest parade in Germany. My
      job was to develop the motor controller. I had never used a microcontroller
      before. An Arduino R3 board was used, but to make it a bit harder I chose to
      program in C instead of using the Arduino framework. I wrote a program without
      libraries to control the stepper motors: <ExternalLink
        href="https://github.com/deanqx/avr-motor-controller"
      >
        avr-motor-controller at Github
      </ExternalLink>. I wouldn't recommend using that library, it's missing the
      ability to power the motor smoothly with PWM.
      <h2>June 2024</h2>
      <p>Built a webapp for a retirement home.</p>
      <h2>February 2024</h2>
      <video
        src="/timeline/2024-02-03_fluffy.mp4"
        alt="2D Game where you play a cloud and dodge airplanes."
        type="video/mp4"
        loading="lazy"
        muted
        autoplay
        loop
        playinline
        width="700"></video>
      My first year in high school (Abitur). In the Java programming class we got
      the task to follow a 2D game tutorial and create a documentation. Retyping the
      tutorial was a bit boring, so I made my own version: <ExternalLink
        href="https://github.com/deanqx/Fluffy"
      >
        Fluffy
      </ExternalLink><br /> Despite my large time investment I got a bad grade for
      it because it didn't follow the tutorial. Anyway, the visitors on Open House
      day loved it.
      <h2>June 2023</h2>
      <p>
        Built a tool to show server temperature and other metrics on a Website.
        This was my first project to use Rust. The backend used <ExternalLink
          href="https://github.com/tokio-rs/axum">Rust axum</ExternalLink
        > and vanilla Javascript was used for the frontend.
      </p>
      <h2>February 2023</h2>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2023-02-28_3d_game.webp"
        alt="Working on a 3D Game"
        width={700}
      />
      In a prototype called <ExternalLink
        href="https://github.com/deanqx/Shadow-Intruder"
      >
        Shadow-Intruder
      </ExternalLink>, I experimented with procedural world generation, path
      finding (using A*), 3D modeling and game mechanics like shooting and
      movement. <br />
      The image shows four generated world tiles with water, grass, and mountains.
      You can see how I was debugging the lighting: I visualized the poly normal vectors
      with a script using different colors. Normals are used to calculate basic lighting.
      The lighting didn't look correct on the border between tiles. To fix this, the
      adjacent polygons had to be included for the normal vector calculation. The
      adjacent polygons are highlighted in pink.<br />
      This was before my higher education (Abitur) and I hadn't learned about vectors
      in school. I watched YouTube tutorials about Linear Algebra.
      <h2>August 2021</h2>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2021-08-07_third_setup.webp"
        alt="Third computer setup"
        width={700}
      />
      New equipment and new projects. I loved and still love the YouTube videos about
      AI learning to play a game. So I wanted to learn how AI works. But I didn't
      want to just use an existing library -- I wanted to know how it worked behind
      the scenes. After all, this wasn't a project that needed to be production-ready.
      Note that I started it before the AI hype from ChatGPT's release in 2022. At
      this time I also learned about Git and GitHub. So I built the C++ library called&nbsp;<ExternalLink
        href="https://github.com/deanqx/Seayon">Seayon</ExternalLink
      > just by following the YouTube series of <ExternalLink
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aircAruvnKk&list=PLZHQObOWTQDNU6R1_67000Dx_ZCJB-3pi"
        >3Blue1Brown on Neural Networks</ExternalLink
      >. My goal was to use it as a trading AI. Spoiler: you can't beat Wall
      Street with a plain Neural Network. In the end I still learned a lot and
      improved my math grades.
      <h2>December 2020</h2>
      <p>
        Built an Android app for a Butcher with multiple shops, so customers can
        read information about a specific product. The product information was
        provided in the form of PDFs.
      </p>
      <h2>June 2019</h2>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2019-05-23_second_setup.webp"
        alt="Second computer setup"
        width={500}
      />
      <p>
        The Minecraft server wasn't really as popular as I hoped, so I abandoned
        it as well as my YouTube channel in 2018. Surprisingly a few friends
        actually starting playing a lot on it in 2020, when I had already lost
        interest. In 2019, I got an actual desk and started learning about Java,
        C# and desktop app development. I liked the idea of cryptocurrency, so I
        built a digital bank (<ExternalLink
          href="https://github.com/deanqx/BankDemo"
          >BankDemo at GitHub
        </ExternalLink>) with a server-client system without encryption. The
        tutorial I followed was about a chat program using Java. After that I
        wanted to look into C#, which I did by building a Steam clone (<ExternalLink
          href="https://github.com/deanqx/ArcadeHub"
        >
          ArcadeHub at GitHub
        </ExternalLink>).
      </p>
      <h2>2017</h2>
      <p>
        Published a <ExternalLink
          href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pCqS7dqVr1Q"
          >YouTube video</ExternalLink
        > about the Minecraft server. I was 12 years old at this point.
      </p>
      <h2>February 2016</h2>
      <img
        src="/timeline/2016-02-08_first_setup.webp"
        alt="First computer setup"
        width={500}
      />
      <p>
        Starting from 2014, I was a big Minecraft fan for the next years. Around
        2016 came the idea to build my own Minecraft server. My advantage was
        that my father ran a technical company and had a Windows server for me
        to use.
      </p>
      <h2>before 2016</h2>
      <p>
        My first contact with things outside the normal user space started with
        a video on YouTube about "hacking" a Wi-Fi password with the Windows
        command line. It wasn't about actual hacking but rather reading the
        passwords of Wi-Fi networks already saved on the computer. After all
        that didn't really matter because it successfully showed me my Wi-Fi
        password, and I was convinced <code>cmd.exe</code> was some kind of hacker
        tool. I wanted to learn more, so I searched on YouTube and found this series:&nbsp;<ExternalLink
          href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CvNvUlQ6Knw"
        >
          CMD Tutorial Deutsch von ANSPictures (2010)
        </ExternalLink>
      </p>
      <h2>November 2014</h2>
      <p>
        Start of YouTube channel: Recorded gaming videos with Phone and later
        learned about screen recording with OBS.
      </p>
    </main>
    <Footer />

    <style>
      main {
        max-width: 1024px;
      }

      video,
      img {
        display: block;
        margin-left: auto;
        margin-right: auto;
        margin-bottom: 1em;
      }

      .circuit-background {
        mask-image: url("/timeline/2026-03-06_circuit.svg");
        mask-size: contain;
        mask-position: top;
        mask-repeat: no-repeat;
        background-color: currentcolor;
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        z-index: -1;
        margin-top: 1lh;
      }

      .image-grid {
        margin-top: 5em;
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
        justify-items: center;
        gap: 2em;
        margin-bottom: 1em;
      }

      .image-grid img {
        height: 100%;
      }

      @media (width <= 720px) {
        .image-grid {
          grid-template-columns: 1fr;
          gap: 0.5em;
        }
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
